\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Consider $k\geq2$ runners starting together at $0$ on the circle $\mathbb R/\mathbb Z$ of circumference $1$, with pairwise distinct constant real speeds $v_1,\ldots,v_k$. The position of runner $i$ at time $t$ is $tv_i$ modulo $1$. For $x\in\mathbb R$ let $\lVert x\rVert_{\mathbb R/\mathbb Z}=\min_{m\in\mathbb Z}|x-m|$, the shorter circular distance from $x$ to $0$. Different runners may have different lonely times.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every such $k$ and speed tuple, does each runner $i\in\{1,\ldots,k\}$ have a time $t\geq0$ such that
\[
\lVert t(v_i-v_j)\rVert_{\mathbb R/\mathbb Z}\geq\frac1k
\qquad\text{for all }j\ne i?
\] \href{https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.14111}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Does each runner eventually become at least one divided by the number of runners away from every other runner on the unit circular track?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 1320 (COMB-002). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Matthieu Rosenfeld, \emph{The lonely runner conjecture holds for eight runners}, arXiv:2509.14111v2 (2025), Section 1, Conjectures 1 and 2 and the paragraph between them. \url{https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.14111}.
\end{description}
